==========================================================

DONOR TRUST QUOTA APPLICATION

ENVIRONMENT CONFIGURATION TEMPLATE

File: .env.example

Version: 1.3.0

==========================================================

PURPOSE

This file documents the environment variables used to configure

the Donor Trust Quota Application and its supporting services.

It provides a consistent configuration reference for local

development, automated testing, staging and production deployment.

IMPORTANT PROJECT DISCLAIMER

This project is an independent application-preparation service.

It is NOT an official WBMCC submission portal, government service,

counselling authority or admission-allocation system.

A reference generated by this application does not establish

official submission, eligibility, approval or seat allocation.

–––––––––––––––––––––––––––––

LOCAL DEVELOPMENT SETUP

–––––––––––––––––––––––––––––

1. Copy this template to a local .env file:

cp .env.example .env

2. Populate required values using your local environment.

3. Install dependencies:

npm ci

4. Run the application:

npm run dev

5. Run validation and tests:

npm run verify

Use these commands only if the corresponding scripts exist

in package.json and are compatible with your project.

SECRET MANAGEMENT

- Never commit .env files, passwords or production credentials.

- Commit .env.example with placeholder or blank secret values.

- Configure production variables through the hosting provider.

- Use distinct, cryptographically random secrets for each purpose.

- Rotate any credential that has been exposed.

CONFIGURATION CONTRACT

A variable has no effect merely because it appears in this file.

Application code, installed packages and deployment configuration

must explicitly read, validate and enforce each supported setting.

PRIVACY AND SECURITY PRINCIPLES

- Collect only information necessary for the stated purpose.

- Never log applicant payloads, documents, tokens or cookies.

- Never place credentials or sensitive personal information in URLs.

- Keep identity documents and generated PDFs in private storage.

- Authenticate and authorize every protected operation.

- Enforce validation and consent checks on the server.

- Document retention, deletion, backup and incident procedures.

- Do not enable production submissions before security testing.

==========================================================

1. APPLICATION IDENTITY AND RUNTIME

==========================================================

NODE_ENV

Controls the application runtime environment. Production mode

should be used for the deployed service. Development mode is

intended for local development and debugging.

APP_NAME

Human-readable application name for supported logs, messages

and administrative displays. This is not an authentication value.

APP_BASE_URL

Canonical base URL of the application. Use the actual deployed

HTTPS origin in production. Validate this value before using it

to construct redirects, links or absolute callback URLs.

HOST

Network interface on which the local Node.js server listens.

A loopback address limits local development access. A hosted

deployment may require binding to the interface expected by

the platform.

PORT

TCP port used by the HTTP server. The application must read

this value and listen on the configured port.

NODE_ENV=development
APP_NAME="Donor Trust Quota Application"
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=3000

==========================================================

2. POSTGRESQL DATABASE AND CONNECTION POOL

==========================================================

DATABASE_URL

PostgreSQL connection string supplied by the database provider.

It may contain a username, password, hostname, port and database

name. Treat the entire connection string as a sensitive secret.

DB_SSL

Controls whether TLS is requested by the application’s database

connection configuration. The exact value format and behavior

must match the implementation in server.js.

Production database providers may require TLS and certificate

verification. Do not disable certificate verification merely

to work around connection errors.

DB_POOL_MIN

Minimum number of database connections maintained by the pool,

if the selected pool implementation supports this setting.

DB_POOL_MAX

Maximum number of simultaneous pooled database connections.

Choose a limit compatible with the provider’s connection quota

and the number of deployed application instances.

DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS

Maximum permitted wait for a new database connection.

DB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS

Time before an idle pooled connection may be released.

DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS

Intended query execution timeout. The application must implement

this using a supported PostgreSQL or driver configuration.

These settings do not create tables or apply migrations.

Manage database schema changes through your existing migration

system. Apply only new, forward-compatible migrations.

DATABASE_URL=
DB_SSL=false
DB_POOL_MIN=0
DB_POOL_MAX=10
DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
DB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

==========================================================

3. AUTHENTICATION, SESSION MANAGEMENT AND ADMIN ACCESS

==========================================================

SESSION_SECRET

Secret used by the application’s session mechanism, if present.

Generate a strong, unpredictable value using a cryptographic

random number generator. Do not reuse database or admin secrets.

ADMIN_API_TOKEN

Server-side credential for the protected administrative API,

if the application uses static bearer-token authentication.

Store it only in a protected environment configuration.

SESSION_COOKIE_NAME

Name assigned to the application’s session cookie.

SESSION_TTL_MS

Intended session lifetime in milliseconds. The session

implementation must enforce expiration and invalidation.

SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE

When enabled, instructs the supported cookie configuration

to transmit the cookie only over HTTPS.

SESSION_COOKIE_HTTP_ONLY

Prevents browser JavaScript from reading the cookie when the

application correctly configures the HttpOnly attribute.

SESSION_COOKIE_SAME_SITE

Controls cross-site cookie behavior. Select a value compatible

with the application’s login and navigation requirements.

Local HTTP development may require Secure=false. Production

deployments using HTTPS should use Secure=true.

A cookie setting does not itself create a session system.

Ensure session storage, expiration, logout and authentication

checks are implemented and tested.

SESSION_SECRET=
ADMIN_API_TOKEN=

SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=donor_quota_session
SESSION_TTL_MS=28800000

SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=false
SESSION_COOKIE_HTTP_ONLY=true
SESSION_COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax

==========================================================

4. NETWORK SECURITY, CORS AND REQUEST LIMITS

==========================================================

TRUST_PROXY

Controls Express proxy trust behavior when implemented by the

application. Incorrect settings can allow forged forwarded

headers to affect client-IP detection and security controls.

Enable proxy trust only after verifying the actual hosting

topology and configuring the appropriate trust boundary.

CORS_ORIGIN

Permitted browser origin or origins. An origin consists of the

scheme, hostname and optional port, without a URL path.

If comma-separated values are supported, each origin must be

parsed and checked against an explicit allowlist. Do not use

a wildcard with credentialed browser requests.

RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS

Duration of the general rate-limiting window in milliseconds.

RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS

Maximum requests permitted per client during that window,

subject to the actual rate-limiting implementation.

AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS

Intended request limit for authentication-sensitive endpoints.

Apply stricter controls to login, token verification and other

endpoints that could be abused for credential guessing.

JSON_BODY_LIMIT

Maximum accepted JSON request-body size. This does not replace

separate multipart upload limits or reverse-proxy limits.

REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS

Intended maximum processing time for a request, if enforced.

HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS

Intended timeout for receiving complete HTTP request headers.

KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS

Intended HTTP keep-alive connection timeout.

CSRF_PROTECTION_ENABLED

Enables the application’s CSRF protection only if the relevant

middleware and token/origin validation are implemented.

CORS and SameSite cookies alone are not universal CSRF defenses.

TRUST_PROXY=false
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=900000
RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=60
AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=10

JSON_BODY_LIMIT=100kb
REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS=35000
KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

CSRF_PROTECTION_ENABLED=false

==========================================================

5. PRIVATE FILE STORAGE AND DOCUMENT UPLOADS

==========================================================

UPLOAD_DIR

Local directory for uploaded files when local filesystem storage

is selected. Keep it outside the public web root.

MAX_FILE_SIZE_MB

Intended maximum size of an individual uploaded file.

Enforce this in the upload middleware and storage layer.

MAX_FILES_PER_APPLICATION

Intended maximum number of files associated with one application.

Enforce the limit on the server rather than relying on the browser.

ALLOWED_UPLOAD_MIME_TYPES

Comma-separated allowlist of permitted content types, if supported.

The server must validate file signatures and content as appropriate.

Browser-supplied MIME types and filename extensions are not reliable

proof of a file’s actual format.

UPLOADS_PUBLICLY_ACCESSIBLE

Intended policy for document accessibility. Private applicant

documents must not be exposed through public static file serving.

FILE_STORAGE_PROVIDER

Selects the storage adapter implemented by the application.

"local" is suitable only where the filesystem is sufficiently

protected, persistent and backed up. Hosted filesystems may be

ephemeral, so production storage requirements must be reviewed.

FILE_STORAGE_BUCKET

Private object-storage bucket or container name, if applicable.

FILE_STORAGE_REGION

Storage region or provider-specific location.

FILE_STORAGE_ENDPOINT

Optional custom endpoint for a compatible object-storage provider.

FILE_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID

Server-side storage access identifier, if required by the provider.

FILE_STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY

Server-side storage secret. Never expose it to browser code.

Before accepting real documents, implement authorization for every

upload and download, file-size limits, content validation, storage

isolation and an appropriate malware scanning or quarantine process.

UPLOAD_DIR=private-uploads
MAX_FILE_SIZE_MB=10
MAX_FILES_PER_APPLICATION=15

ALLOWED_UPLOAD_MIME_TYPES=application/pdf,image/jpeg,image/png
UPLOADS_PUBLICLY_ACCESSIBLE=false

FILE_STORAGE_PROVIDER=local
FILE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
FILE_STORAGE_REGION=
FILE_STORAGE_ENDPOINT=
FILE_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
FILE_STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

==========================================================

6. PDF GENERATION AND PRIVATE DOCUMENT OUTPUT

==========================================================

PDF_OUTPUT_DIR

Local output directory for generated PDF files. Keep generated

applicant records outside the public web root.

PDF_FILENAME_PREFIX

Prefix for generated filenames. Do not include applicant names,

identity numbers, email addresses or other sensitive information

in filenames.

PDF_INCLUDE_SUBMISSION_REFERENCE

Controls whether the application’s own reference is included

in generated PDFs, if implemented. This reference is not proof

of official submission to WBMCC.

Generated PDFs may contain sensitive personal information.

Authorize every download and avoid predictable public URLs.

PDF_OUTPUT_DIR=private-generated
PDF_FILENAME_PREFIX=donor-quota-application
PDF_INCLUDE_SUBMISSION_REFERENCE=true

==========================================================

7. APPLICATION WORKFLOW, CONSENT AND SUBMISSION

==========================================================

APPLICATION_REFERENCE_PREFIX

Prefix for internally generated application references.

The reference identifies a record within this service only.

APPLICATION_DRAFT_TTL_DAYS

Intended lifetime for incomplete drafts. Actual expiration

requires a cleanup or expiration mechanism and a documented

retention policy.

REQUIRE_CONSENT_BEFORE_SUBMISSION

Requires the application to check valid consent records before

accepting finalization, when implemented in server-side logic.

Store the consent text/version, acceptance timestamp and other

necessary evidence without collecting excessive metadata.

APPLICATION_SUBMISSION_ENABLED

Operational feature switch for the application’s own finalization

endpoint. Keep disabled until authentication, authorization,

validation, consent handling, database transactions and error

recovery have been tested together.

MAX_APPLICATIONS_PER_USER

Intended limit on the number of application records associated

with one authenticated user. This requires a reliable user identity

and server-side enforcement; it is not meaningful for anonymous

users without an appropriate identity mechanism.

Generate a completion reference only after the database transaction

succeeds. Clearly distinguish application-preparation completion

from any official application or counselling submission.

APPLICATION_REFERENCE_PREFIX=DTQ
APPLICATION_DRAFT_TTL_DAYS=30
REQUIRE_CONSENT_BEFORE_SUBMISSION=true
APPLICATION_SUBMISSION_ENABLED=false
MAX_APPLICATIONS_PER_USER=5

==========================================================

8. PRIVACY-CONSCIOUS LOGGING AND OBSERVABILITY

==========================================================

LOG_LEVEL

Minimum severity recorded by the logger, where supported.

Use a controlled level suitable for the environment.

LOG_REDACT_SENSITIVE_DATA

Intended to enable redaction of sensitive values. Redaction must

be implemented and verified in the actual logging pipeline.

LOG_REQUEST_BODIES

Controls request-body logging only if the application implements

this switch. Keep disabled for applicant-facing endpoints.

LOG_RESPONSE_BODIES

Controls response-body logging only if implemented. Keep disabled

to avoid recording personal data, tokens or confidential records.

Never log authorization headers, cookies, session IDs, passwords,

raw access tokens, applicant payloads, identity numbers, signatures,

document contents or database connection strings.

Prefer structured logs with an explicit allowlist of safe fields.

Avoid logging raw exceptions when they may contain credentials.

LOG_LEVEL=info
LOG_REDACT_SENSITIVE_DATA=true
LOG_REQUEST_BODIES=false
LOG_RESPONSE_BODIES=false

==========================================================

9. OPTIONAL EMAIL NOTIFICATIONS

==========================================================

EMAIL_PROVIDER

Identifier for the email provider supported by the application.

EMAIL_FROM

Configured sender address. Use an address authorized by the

selected provider and the domain’s email authentication policy.

EMAIL_API_KEY

Provider credential. Keep blank until a provider is selected

and store the production value in the deployment secret manager.

Notifications should contain only the minimum information needed.

Do not include identity documents, passwords, access tokens or

unnecessary applicant details in email messages.

EMAIL_PROVIDER=
EMAIL_FROM=
EMAIL_API_KEY=

==========================================================

10. HEALTH CHECKS AND SERVICE READINESS

==========================================================

HEALTH_CHECK_ENABLED

Enables the basic health endpoint only if implemented.

/health

Intended to report basic application process health.

It should not expose database URLs, secrets, internal stack traces

or personal information.

/ready

Intended to report whether the dependencies required to serve

requests are ready. Database connectivity checks should return

minimal information and must not disclose connection details.

Health endpoints are not substitutes for authentication,

application monitoring or database recovery procedures.

HEALTH_CHECK_ENABLED=true

==========================================================

11. DATA RETENTION, DELETION AND PRIVACY

==========================================================

AUDIT_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS

Proposed retention period for audit events, subject to applicable

legal, security and operational requirements.

UPLOAD_RETENTION_DAYS

Proposed retention period for stored uploads, subject to the

documented purpose, active applications and applicable obligations.

DELETE_EXPIRED_UPLOADS

Enables an automated deletion process only if the application

implements one. Keep disabled until retention rules, authorization,

backups and recovery requirements have been reviewed.

Retention settings are not automatic deletion commands by themselves.

Any cleanup job must be access-controlled, observable and tested.

Consider database records, object storage, replicas, backups and

deletion audit records when designing the lifecycle.

AUDIT_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=180
UPLOAD_RETENTION_DAYS=365
DELETE_EXPIRED_UPLOADS=false

==========================================================

12. OPERATIONAL CONTROLS AND MAINTENANCE

==========================================================

MAINTENANCE_MODE

Intended switch for temporarily restricting ordinary application

operations during maintenance. Define which routes remain available

and return a suitable response when maintenance mode is active.

APPLICATION_SUBMISSION_ENABLED

Configured above in Section 7. Keep disabled until the complete

submission workflow is ready for controlled testing.

Operational switches must be validated at startup. Invalid values

should produce a clear configuration error rather than silently

weakening security or changing application behavior.

MAINTENANCE_MODE=false

==========================================================

13. DEPLOYMENT AND CONFIGURATION VALIDATION

==========================================================

Before deployment:

[ ] Confirm that the runtime uses the intended Node.js version.

[ ] Confirm DATABASE_URL points to the intended PostgreSQL database.

[ ] Confirm TLS behavior matches the database provider.

[ ] Confirm every required environment variable is validated.

[ ] Confirm production secrets are configured outside Git.

[ ] Confirm TRUST_PROXY matches the hosting topology.

[ ] Confirm CORS_ORIGIN matches the deployed frontend origin.

[ ] Confirm private files cannot be accessed without authorization.

[ ] Confirm consent is validated on the server.

[ ] Confirm migrations are applied in order and tracked.

[ ] Confirm backups, retention and deletion procedures are defined.

[ ] Test authorization failures, invalid inputs and database errors.

[ ] Test with fictional applicant data before production use.

DATABASE COMPATIBILITY

This template is intended for a Node.js application using PostgreSQL

and the "pg" package. It is not a direct configuration for Cloudflare

D1 or SQLite. Those environments require their own database adapter

and compatible migrations.

MIGRATION POLICY

Do not replace or rerun migrations that have already been applied.

Introduce schema improvements through new, forward-only migrations

compatible with the actual database and current schema version.

==========================================================

END OF ENVIRONMENT CONFIGURATION TEMPLATE

